WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.820
So, there's this fantastic Wikipedia list 
descriptively titled:

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:06.000
Wikipedia:Unusual Articles.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
I just remember it as, That Wikipedia List

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
It's a collection of hundreds of articles about unusual, unexpected, and unbelievable subjects.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.000
No matter what link you click, it's
bound to be interesting.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.500
For that reason, every week, I'm going
to click on a new link,

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.000
and teach you a little more about our amazing world.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Today, we're going to take a trip.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.800
We're going to slip the surly bonds of earth.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.000
Up into the heavens.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.000
We're travelling through the galaxy

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.500
to learn about timekeeping, on the red planet.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.420
Mars.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.000
But before we can understand timekeeping on Mars,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.500
we first have to understand timekeeping on Earth.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.500
Dammit, ughh, can we.. can we turn this thing around?

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.000
Alright, so this is the Greenwich Observatory, in England.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Greenwich is known for being the site
of the Prime Meridian.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
For all intents and purposes, this is the
longitudinal center of Earth.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.500
For simplicity's sake, we'll say that
time is determined based on the Sun.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:03.960
This isn't exactly true though,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.000
so I have whole another video that
explains time in more detail.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.500
When the sun is highest in the sky at any given location, we call that noon.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.200
And the rest of the day's time, is relative to this.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.620
So now let's go south,

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.500
to the intersection between the equator,
and the prime meridian.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.000
We find ourselves in the middle of the Gulf of Guinea.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Similar to how the prime meridian is the longitudinal center of the Earth,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.000
the equator is the latitudinal center of the Earth.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.100
At this point of 0° Longitude and 0° Latitude,

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.500
it's noon, because the Sun is at it's highest point in the sky.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.600
Great, now if we go 1,035 miles east

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.980
we find ourselves in the lovely village of Etoumbi,

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:42.600
in the Republic of the Congo.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.000
In Etoumbi, the time is 1PM,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.000
because the Sun reached it's highest point an hour ago.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.000
This is why we have time zones.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.280
Since the Earth rotates once every 24 hours,

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.000
there are 24 time zones.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.340
But wait, I lied,

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.940
it doesn't take 24 hours for the earth to rotate once,

00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:58.440
it takes,

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.500
That's because there are really 2 definitions of a day.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.000
The conventional definition is a Solar Day.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.300
The time between two noons when the Sun is highest in the sky.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.000
The more scientific definition is a Sidereal Day.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.000
That's the time it takes for the Earth to rotate 360° around it's axis

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
and therefore the time it takes for the same star to appear in the same spot

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.000
in the sky from one day to the next.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.000
There's a difference between
Sidereal Days and Solar Days

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.700
because the Earth both on it's axis and around the Sun.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.000
So, watch this.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.500
Let's put a marker on a spot on the Earth and fast forward one sidereal day.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.500
You'll notice that the same spot is not yet the closest point

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.000
on Earth to the Sun because the Earth also rotated around the Sun.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.400
To bring the marker closest to the Sun,

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.000
we'll need to advance 4 minutes forward, and now we're at one Solar Day.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Those 4 minutes add up conveniently to just about 24 hours over one year.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.000
So that means there's one more Sidereal Day per year than Solar Day.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.000
Alright, now we can go to Mars.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:01.580
That was quick.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.300
So Mars is a pretty similar planet to Earth and time works in a pretty similar way.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.000
It has a prime meridian right... here.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.500
Strangely, astronomers chose this prime meridian before Earth's was chosen.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.800
Using the mean solar time at the prime meridian,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.800
there's a coordinated Martian time known as MTC.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.500
But, for the most part any rovers on Mars

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.000
just set their clocks using the solar
time of their landing site.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.000
This is actually pretty similar to how towns on Earth

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.000
set their time before coordinated time
zones were created.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.000
Each town on Earth set their time
based on their own solar noon.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.000
But this created difficulties when rapid transit between places was invented

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.600
since no-one really knew what time it was.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.420
If you want to learn more about this,

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.580
you can once again go check out my 
other video on time.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.000
Since there have only ever been 11
operational space crafts on Mars,

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.500
having different time standards based on solar time hasn't yet caused any problems.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.500
It does mean, however, that even the seconds aren't coordinated between different rovers.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:05.600
What does cause a bit of a problem though, is that a Martian day is slightly longer than an Earth day.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.300
Anyone who's watched The Martian knows that a solar day on Mars is called a Sol.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.500
Rovers on Mars generally only work during the day

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.500
partially because some are solar powered,

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:26.000
and partially because much of the work is based on observation from cameras which need light.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.500
Therefore, the teams on Earth controlling rovers have to live their

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:31.500
lives based on Martian time.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:35.500
Their work shift essentially moves forward 40 minutes every day to coordinate

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.500
with the time that it's light on Mars.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.000
Deborah Bass, a scientist from the Jet Propulsion Laboratory

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.000
who worked on the Spirit and Opportunity programs, said that

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.500
Scientists who work on rovers sometimes wear two watches,

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.000
one for Martian time and one for Earth time.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.660
So, if we do go colonize Mars,

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.000
we'll want to create time zones and actually use the standardized time.

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:07.500
Having different time standards at different places always creates problems,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.000
especially for computers and communications.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.500
This, of course, is just one of many details that we'll have to figure out before we go live on the red planet.

